  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nWe will use a combination of deterministic optimization and stochastic exploration. Unlike the parent's approach, we will model the sequence as a piecewise smooth function, optimizing the coefficients in a multi-resolution framework. The core idea is to minimize the effective peak concentration of the autocorrelation while maximizing the sum of coefficients, which generally leads to lower objective values. We use the fact that higher frequency oscillations often allow for better balancing between the numerator (max convolution) and denominator (sum of coefficients squared) in the objective function.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nThe objective function is:\n    objective = 2 * n * max_k(convolve(sequence, mode=\"full\")[k]) / (sum(sequence))^2\n\nConstraints:\n    sum(sequence) >= 0.01\n    All elements non-negative\n\nWe will optimize two different functional forms:\n    Form A: Piecewise constant, $4^\\textrm{th}$-order Butterworth filter components repeated exponentially.\n    Form B: Bessel filter coefficients arranged in a fractal pattern.\n\nThe optimizer will consider sequences of length 1024, 2048, and 4096. The optimization proceeds in two phases:\n    Phase 1: Stochastic sampling and local optimization using gradient descent.\n    Phase 2: Global coordinate descent with ARMA structure refinement.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nWe will implement the following steps:\n\n    a. Initialization:\n        - If a parent sequence is provided (height_sequence_1), use it as base.\n        - Otherwise, generate a sequence using Form B at length 1024.\n\n    b. Phase 1: Internal Optimization (150 seconds)\n        - Use gradient descent to optimize sequences of length 1024, 2048, and 4096.\n        - For each length:\n            - Generate multiple starting points (Form A and Form B mixtures).\n            - Use adaptive step sizes and learning rates.\n            - Perform coordinate-wise descent emphasizing oscillatory patterns.\n\n    c. Phase 2: ARMAted Cross-Dimensional Projection (600 seconds)\n        - Perform projections of the best solution found so far across dimensions.\n        - Use ARMA models to simulate the autocorrelation behavior.\n        - Introduce controlled perturbations of up to 10% magnitude.\n        - Maintain the BJJ transition (sum normalization).\n\n    d. Phase 3: Multi-Wavelet Deletion (600 seconds)\n        - Use a wavelet-based approach to identify correlation gaps.\n        - Insert a spike-reduced sequence where overdensity exists.\n        - Use a threshold normalization (0.01 max sum).\n\n    e. Final Check (120 seconds)\n        - Reconvolve the best sequence and validate.\n        - Compare against previous best and return if improved.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n    Primary search space dimensions: [1024, 2048, 4096]\n\n    Optimization variants:\n        - Gradient-Based with exponential mixing\n        - ARMA projection to higher/lower dimensions\n        - Wavelet decomposition with significance thresholding\n\n    These variations are chosen as the core representation in the optimization process, cycling through different configurations.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Error Control:\n    Total time: 1800 seconds\n\n        - Phase 1: 150 seconds total (50 seconds per length)\n        - Phase 2: 600 seconds total (200 seconds per length)\n        - Phase 3: 600 seconds total (200 seconds per length)\n        - Final Check: 100 seconds total\n\n    Error handling:\n        - If any calculation produces NaN/inf, skip it.\n        - Maintain a separate memory buffer for the best phase1 candidate.\n        - Exponential backoff if optimization stalls.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n    The final sequence must pass through:\n\n        a. Non-negative enforcement (all >=0)\n        b. Sum threshold (>=0.01)\n        c. Convolution finite value\n\n    Then the objective is computed and compared.\n\n    If the best candidate from any phase is validated, return it with its objective value. Otherwise, fall back to the parent sequence if available.\n\n    The implementation will:\n\n        - Use numpy for linear algebra.\n        - Use scipy.stats for distributions.\n        - Use time.monotonic for elapsed time.",
